# Warp operating rules

- `.warp/beam.json` is the source of truth. Update it only through `scripts/beam.py` or `scripts/scan.py`.
- Do not dispatch unless `runState` is `running`.
- Do not start a ticket until every dep is merged and every blocking upstream gate is green.
- Do not run two active tickets whose lock paths overlap.
- The orchestrator is the only merger. It dispatches, merges, and tracks, and it writes no ticket product code. Shuttles and Reed do not merge and do not push to the base branch.
- The only concurrency cap is `maxAgents`. A project may set 18. Do not hardcode a cap.
- One ticket, one branch, one isolated checkout. Cloud is one new Agent per ticket: a separate top-level cloud agent. Own conversation, own VM, own checkout. `checkout.py launch` does not create the branch. It prints an IMPLEMENT prompt that contains the claim: ticket id, Jira key, locks, acceptance, and branch `warp/<id>-<jira>`. The parent starts one new Agent with that prompt. Clone main so `.cursor` rules load, then create that branch from the main it cloned. It writes `.warp/tickets/<id>/` and pushes. The parent reads that folder later. A beam file does not have to exist on the branch before the Agent starts. If that branch already exists and is only the beam commit, the prompt says to start from latest main and not to keep that stale beam as the working tree. Do not start a Subagent. A Subagent is a child spawned inside the orchestrator's turn (Task / subagent). It shares the parent session and checkout. Forbidden for tickets. Do not use the Task tool for a ticket. Do not implement the ticket in this turn. The plugin has no cloud-agent API. Local is one git worktree (`git worktree add` / `git worktree remove`) of that ticket's branch, not a Subagent inside the orchestrator checkout. Never two tickets in one VM or one worktree. Never two Agents on one ticket. The beam stores the agent id or the worktree path.
- Ready means every dependency and every `after` id is merged on the base branch, and no lock path overlaps a lock held by an in-flight ticket. Overlap is the same path or a parent folder. Use the ticket's full lock list. An open or green pull request is not merged.
- Dispatch at start and after every merge, failure, and freed slot. Starred and priority first, then lowest rank, until `maxAgents` are in flight. Do not hold a slot for a ticket that is not ready. Do not wait for a whole dependency level.
- Opening a pull request means the Shuttle finished. The slot stays occupied until the GitHub or Bitbucket check rollup is green. Bugbot runs on the pull request after push, not inside the VM. Locks stay until merge or park. `checkCommand` empty means Bugbot and CI as configured. `make ci` is kept as the full check command. A red result is stored on `pr.check` and the ticket is sent back with the log (`send-back <id> fix` and a `start` line). The third red parks. The name alone is not a red check. The gate stays while that check is red. `appendOnlyPaths` is empty by default. A conflict there keeps both sides, every added line. Any other conflict is sent back. Do not hand-merge it. `mergeQueue` defaults to false. When it is true, or GitHub reports a merge queue, enqueue the pull request. A direct merge that branch protection rejects is not marked merged. `state_commit.py commit` commits the beam, journal, STATUS, and BOARD and strips tokens, cost, API keys, and webhook URLs. `.warp/config.yaml` stays gitignored. On a cloud runner, `/warp-start` refuses when `transitionJiraIssue`, `addOrEditJiraIssueComment`, or `slack_send_message` is missing from the MCP allow list. `--force` starts anyway. A local runner does not block.
- The merge queue is serial: starred first, then lowest rank. Rebase, run the check, merge, delete the branch, dispatch. Sizes outside `autoMergeSizes` still wait for `/warp-proceed` or `warp:proceed`. The third red check parks the ticket, releases its locks, and leaves the branch and pull request open. A red base branch stops merging and a fix is dispatched ahead of every rank. Restart classifies merged, in flight, and pending from git.
- On start or resume, rebuild from git: merged on the base branch, in flight when a branch or pull request is open, otherwise pending. Done means every ticket is merged or explicitly parked, and the base branch is green.
- There is no per-person cap. The only concurrency cap is `maxAgents`.
- A size in `autoMergeSizes` auto-merges after Bugbot and CI are green, including L and XL when they are listed. L and XL are not special. The default list is S, M, so those wait for a provider approval or `warp:proceed <id>` unless you add them. QA Ready is not set before Bugbot is clean.
- The git provider is `gitProvider` in `.warp/config.yaml` (`auto`, `github`, or `bitbucket`). `pushMerge: false` means local branches and a local merge, no push and no pull request.
- Coding model comes from `.warp/config.yaml`. Default `claude-sonnet-5-5-high` (Claude Sonnet 5.5 High). The slug must match the Cursor model picker.
- Jira, GitHub, Bitbucket, Slack, and Teams go through Cursor's connected MCP servers, or `gh` for GitHub when `ghCli` is true. If none is usable, fall back to local-only.
- When `beam.py set` prints `jira: MUST DO`, do every action in `.warp/jira-todo.json` in that turn, then `jira_sync.py record` and `record-comment`. A later failure goes to `.warp/outbox.md` and Herald and does not stop that ticket. The first claimed ticket of a run is different: if Jira says the issue was not found, or no real issue key can be resolved, release the claim back to `queued` and stop the run. `jiraTransition: false` does not stop the run.
- The listener is a Subagent of the parent; each ticket is a new Agent. The listener shares the parent's session and checkout, reads the live beam, and returns inbound `warp:` commands in the same turn. It is not a separate Agent. One listener Subagent per parent turn. It dies when that turn ends, so the parent does not end the turn while the run is running and any ticket is claimed, in review, awaiting checks, or queued. Each pass starts one listener, reads `warp:` commands, fetches `.warp/tickets/<id>/` for in-flight tickets, patches the live beam, dispatches a ready slot with the IMPLEMENT prompt, pushes the beam, and passes again. A launch refusal is not idle. The parent may exit when the run is paused or stopped, or when nothing is claimed, nothing is queued, and nothing is awaiting checks. Pause and stop still sync state, and then the listener may stop. A running flag from the previous turn does not block the next start. The listener does not implement tickets, does not merge, and does not write product code. The parent applies proceed, pause, stop, and retry, and patches the live beam. Do not start a Subagent for a ticket. Do not use the Task tool for a ticket.
- Close the window, open a new one, `/warp-start`. State comes from main plus ticket folders. Every merge commits the live `.warp/beam.json`, journal, and board onto main. The ticket branch's beam is dropped. The end of each parent tick pushes that same beam. Tokens, cost, API keys, and webhook URLs are stripped. `.warp/config.yaml` is not committed. `/warp-update-state` loads main, patches `.warp/tickets/<id>/` only, and puts back pause, stop, claims, and runState the parent has that main does not. `/warp-pause` and `/warp-stop` run that sync before they return.
- A Shuttle writes `lastSeenAt` with `beam.py heartbeat`. The listener writes it with `inbound.py heartbeat` during the parent turn. `beam.py watchdog` runs on every tick and on start and resume. A fresh Shuttle heartbeat is left alone. Pause and stop do not start a replacement. The listener is not replaced by the watchdog. A dead Shuttle becomes `recovering` on the same branch. Past `maxRecoveries` the alarm is `worker-died`. There is no process table. Plugin hooks do not run on cloud runners.
- The parent tick repairs `lock-escape` only, one ticket at a time, every `alarmRepairMinutes` (default 15). The repair widens that ticket's locks to the paths that escaped. It waits when an in-flight ticket holds one of those paths. Past `maxAlarmRepairs` (default 3) the alarm stays. `bugbot-failed`, `worker-died`, `stuck`, `ci-red`, and `gate-red` are left alone. Pause and stop do not repair. The listener does not start the repair Agent.
- A pending or stale-red gate clears when every member is merged or done and no check is actually red, then the tick runs. G1 with members P-002, P-003, P-004, and P-018 stores `members merged: P-002, P-003, P-004, P-018`. Herald posts `G1 pending cleared. Members merged. Tick ran.` A green gate stays green. Pause and stop do not recompute.
- `/warp-upgrade` replaces `.cursor/plugins/warp`. Run it in the repo that has Warp installed. When the source is a git checkout, it fetches the default branch. If the fetch fails, it prints `fetch failed` and `keeping the installed copy`. It does not overwrite `.warp/config.yaml` or the beam. It prints `Warp vX.Y.Z`. Reload Cursor, then confirm with `/warp-version`. `/warp-init` does not upgrade an existing copy. This command does. `--force` is not an upgrade flag. The flags are `--root` and `--source`.
 Init and upgrade write `.cursor/commands/warp-upgrade.md` from `commands/warp-upgrade.md` so a reload lists `/warp-upgrade` when the plugin command index still omits that path.